From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hbadi <badi.hamid@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 01:09:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Export a CMake package and an MSBuild property sheet

An application consuming sparselizard had to restate by hand what the build
already knows: three include directories, the compiler settings PETSc needs,
the link line, and the directories the loader has to search. Nothing published
that, so every consumer rediscovered it, and getting one item wrong fails in
ways that do not name the cause.

install(TARGETS) carried no EXPORT, so find_package(sparselizard) could not
work at all. Export the target and ship a config file, so an application writes

    find_package(sparselizard REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE sparselizard::sparselizard)

The include directories are wrapped in BUILD_INTERFACE/INSTALL_INTERFACE, as
an export refuses build-tree paths. PETSc, SLEPc and BLAS are PUBLIC link
dependencies and are found again by the config file, seeded with the locations
used at build time; each seed is a plain cache entry, so -DPETSC_DIR=... still
wins. The BLAS is recorded rather than searched for again, since locating
oneMKL normally requires its setvars script to have been sourced first, which
an application building against an already-built sparselizard has no reason to
have done. FindCBLAS now honours a caller-provided BLAS_LIBRARIES for that.

A .vcxproj cannot read a CMake package, so the same information is emitted a
second time as an MSBuild property sheet, generated from the same variables as
sparselizard-env.cmd so the three cannot drift. Adding it through the Property
Manager is the whole of the setup. Beyond the settings it also checks the two
requirements that cannot be relaxed, because both fail silently otherwise:

- The application must use the release C runtime, including in Debug builds.
  sparselizard exchanges std::string and std::vector with it, and the debug CRT
  lays them out differently. Nothing fails at build or link time; objects
  simply arrive corrupted, a mesh name reaching the library empty.
- x64 only. A Win32 build gets unresolved externals that read as a missing
  library.

sparselizard-env.cmd listed the dependency directories but not the one holding
sparselizard's own shared library. The examples get away without it because
they are built into that same directory and the loader searches the one holding
the executable image; a binary built anywhere else does not, and fails with
0xC0000135 naming no module. Add it, for the helper and the property sheet
alike.

project() gains the version the library reports at runtime, 2022.5.11, so the
installed package can be version-checked.

Verified with cl.exe on Windows: a .vcxproj whose only sparselizard setting is
the imported sheet, and a CMakeLists using find_package in a shell where no
Intel environment script was sourced, both build, link and run
examples/capacitance-computing to its expected value. Both checks fire with
their message when deliberately violated.

(cherry picked from commit 357919d3dc41320cbc3379812e6e2142bec0c288)
